# The gobit storefront GraphQL schema.
#
# This file is the CONTRACT and is written by hand; graph/generated.go is
# produced from it (make gen). Like the OpenAPI document it is an INSPECTABLE
# artifact: adding or removing a field shows up in the diff.
#
# Every field in the schema HAS a counterpart in the service.StoreProduct tree
# and that is checked by graph/schema_test.go. The reverse direction is
# deliberately free: we do not have to put every field the service returns into
# the schema, but every field we do put into the schema MUST come from the
# service — otherwise the client is promised a field that will never be filled.
#
# THERE IS NO WRITING. The Mutation type was deliberately not defined: this
# surface was opened for READING only in Phase 10, and the write flows (cart,
# order, payment) rest on contracts already solved on the REST side, such as the
# idempotency header, the error class -> HTTP code mapping and event publishing.
# Putting an empty Mutation type here would mean assuming that someone will add
# writing to it tomorrow.

"Timestamp; RFC 3339 (UTC)."
scalar Time

"""
A loosely typed JSON object whose schema DOES NOT BELONG TO THIS MODULE.

It is used in only two places and both are deliberate: the module's own free
area (metadata) and the records of OTHER modules (priceSet, inventoryItem).
For the rationale see Variant.priceSet.
"""
scalar JSON

"""
The storefront's read surface.

The catalog the queries see is limited to the SALES CHANNELS the request's
publishable key is bound to. The channel IS NOT a query argument and will not
be one: had it been an argument, the filter would stop being an authorization
and turn into a display preference, and a client arriving with any key it
happened to hold could read another storefront's catalog.
"""
type Query {
  """
  Lists the published products with their price and inventory information.

  The arguments overlap ONE TO ONE with service.StoreListOptions; an argument
  the service does not read is NOT here (see schema_test.go). That is also why
  there is no sort argument: the service does not take sorting from outside
  today.

  If limit is not given the service's default applies, and if it exceeds the
  upper bound the service's ceiling applies; a negative value is an error. The
  rule lives in the service itself and IS NOT REPEATED here.

  "after" is the opaque nextCursor of a previous page and is what makes a deep
  page cheap: offset asks the database to walk and DISCARD every row it skips,
  so its cost grows with depth, while a cursor goes into the index condition
  and stays flat. Measured on 52,000 products: the last page costs 34.71 ms by
  offset and 0.08 ms by cursor.

  "after" and "offset" name two different positions and are refused together.
  """
  products(
    limit: Int
    offset: Int
    after: String
    q: String
    """
    The order the listing comes back in. Default: newest.

    Both orders ride the SAME index — it is declared (created_at DESC, id DESC)
    and a b-tree is readable in either direction — so neither is the expensive
    one.

    A cursor belongs to ONE order: an "after" minted under newest is REFUSED
    when sent with oldest, because it names a position in a key space read the
    other way and would otherwise serve the wrong page silently.

    Price and availability are not offered here: they are not columns of the
    catalog at all.
    """
    sort: ProductOrder
    collectionId: ID
    """
    Narrows the listing to a category. The id comes from /store/v1/categories:
    a storefront has the word a shopper clicked, not an id.

    A product in several categories is still returned ONCE.
    """
    categoryId: ID
    """
    Narrows the listing to a tag. The id comes from /store/v1/tags.

    A product carrying several tags is still returned ONCE.
    """
    tagId: ID
    """
    Narrows the listing to the products offering one option VALUE ("red").

    The value comes from the option vocabulary endpoint, which is the one
    vocabulary that returns text rather than ids: an option belongs to exactly
    one product, so an option-value id would name a single product's single
    value and filtering a catalog by one would return at most one product.

    The match is on the FOLDED form and the fold is Go's, never the cluster's
    (ADR 0039): two spellings of one value meet, two DIFFERENT values do not,
    and a script the fold has no ASCII base for is kept rather than dropped —
    so a Cyrillic or CJK catalog gains case-insensitivity instead of losing its
    text. A shopper typing an unaccented approximation of such a value still
    misses, and that is a MISS rather than a merge.

    The title of the axis is not part of it: this asks "offers the value red on
    any axis". Whether two axes named differently are one axis is a merchant's
    data question rather than a rule this framework can pick.
    """
    optionValue: String
    """
    Narrows the listing by availability (ADR 0040).

    A VARIANT is in stock when it is not counted (manageInventory false), OR it
    may be sold past zero (allowBackorder), OR its available quantity is above
    zero; a PRODUCT is in stock when at least one of its variants is. A variant
    that is counted and linked to NO inventory item is not in stock — answering
    otherwise would sell what the shop may not have.

    Both directions are answered: true keeps what can be bought, false keeps
    what cannot, and leaving the argument out filters nothing.

    It cannot be a database predicate — two of its three inputs are this
    module's columns and the third is inventory's number — so the listing
    SCANS the catalog for it. Selecting "count" beside it is refused: the total
    would have to be computed over the whole catalog.
    """
    inStock: Boolean
    """
    Narrows the listing to the products with a BASE price inside a bracket
    (ADR 0041).

    "Base" is the price that belongs to NO price list. There is no default
    price list in this schema and no column that could mark one, so the shop's
    ordinary price is the ABSENCE of a list rather than a list one could name.

    The bracket is compared in the currency it names and never converts: a rate
    is a fact about a moment this repository does not store, and a filter that
    quietly converted would answer differently on two consecutive requests.

    A product on SALE shows the sale price and filters by its base price, so a
    shopper asking for "under 100" may not see something currently selling for
    90. That is the price of a filter that does not move on its own.

    Like inStock, it is answered after the catalog is read, so selecting
    "count" beside it is refused.
    """
    price: PriceFilter
  ): ProductList!

  """
  Returns a single storefront product by identity or by handle.

  EXACTLY ONE of the two must be given; if both or neither are given the
  request is rejected with a validation error.

  For a product that is not published, is deleted or is not visible in the
  request's channels the result is null and the error is of the "not found"
  class: giving away the EXISTENCE of a product sold in another channel with a
  different error would pierce the hiding itself.
  """
  product(id: ID, handle: String): Product
}

"""
The price bracket of a catalog filter: a currency and an open or closed
interval over the BASE price at quantity ONE.

It is an INPUT OBJECT rather than three loose arguments because the three are
one criterion: a bound without a currency is not comparable and a currency
without a bound narrows nothing. Grouping them lets the schema say so in its
own types, and it is bound to the service's own PriceBracket, so there is no
converter between the schema's idea of a bracket and the service's.

The REST listing spells the same three as flat query parameters
(currency_code, min_price, max_price) because a query string has no nesting;
the RULES they must satisfy are validated in the service, once, so both
surfaces refuse the same requests.
"""
input PriceFilter {
  "The ISO 4217 code of the currency the bounds are given in, uppercase."
  currencyCode: String!
  """
  The inclusive lower bound in MINOR units; null is an open end.

  Minor units because that is what pricing stores; a decimal here would mean
  this module deciding how many digits a currency has, which is region's
  answer and not the catalog's.
  """
  min: Int
  "The inclusive upper bound in MINOR units; null is an open end."
  max: Int
}

"A paginated product list; it carries the same envelope as the REST storefront list."
type ProductList {
  items: [Product!]!
  "The TOTAL number of records matching the filter (not the ones on the page)."
  count: Int!
  offset: Int!
  "The page size applied; not the one requested (the service's default/ceiling may have been applied)."
  limit: Int!
  """
  The opaque position to send back as "after" for the next page.

  It is NULL on the last page, and that absence is the end-of-listing signal a
  client walking forward needs — the one thing offset cannot give without also
  paying for the count.
  """
  nextCursor: String
}

"""
A product in the storefront.

Only PUBLISHED products are returned; that is why the "status" and "deletedAt"
fields are NOT in the schema — one of them would always be "published" and the
other always null, and they would carry no information the client could decide
anything on.
"""
type Product {
  id: ID!
  "The readable identity that appears in the storefront address."
  handle: String!
  title: String!
  subtitle: String
  description: String
  thumbnail: String
  isGiftcard: Boolean!
  discountable: Boolean!
  weight: Int
  length: Int
  height: Int
  width: Int
  material: String
  originCountry: String
  collectionId: ID
  """
  The product's TYPE — "book", "t-shirt", "download".

  It is published for the same reason collectionId is: it is a catalog fact a
  storefront displays. There is no typeId FILTER on the listing yet; a filter
  needs an index and a decision of its own, and reading a fact is not the same
  request as narrowing by it.
  """
  typeId: ID
  metadata: JSON
  createdAt: Time!
  updatedAt: Time!
  """
  Whether at least one variant of the product can be bought right now
  (ADR 0040).

  It is the BADGE, and it is on every product body rather than behind an
  argument: the inputs are already fetched for the listing, so the answer is
  arithmetic over records the request reads anyway. A product with NO variants
  is not in stock — there is nothing under it to sell.

  The answer is over the sellable total across ALL locations and is NOT
  regional. Making it regional means deciding which locations serve which
  region, a question nothing in this repository asks yet, and it would be a
  different decision with its own record.
  """
  inStock: Boolean!
  "Sellable units. The price and the inventory are HERE, not at the product level."
  variants: [Variant!]!
  options: [Option!]!
  images: [Image!]!
  tags: [Tag!]!
  categories: [Category!]!
}

"The sellable unit: the thing that enters the cart, is kept in stock and is priced is the variant."
type Variant {
  id: ID!
  productId: ID!
  title: String!
  sku: String
  barcode: String
  ean: String
  upc: String
  manageInventory: Boolean!
  allowBackorder: Boolean!
  weight: Int
  rank: Int!
  metadata: JSON
  createdAt: Time!
  updatedAt: Time!
  """
  Whether this variant can be bought right now (ADR 0040).

  True when it is not counted (manageInventory false), OR it may be sold past
  zero (allowBackorder), OR its available quantity is above zero. A variant
  that is counted while nothing counts it — one with no inventoryItem — is
  NOT in stock: the link is optional, and answering otherwise would be a guess
  in the direction of selling something the shop may not have.

  It is COMPUTED on every read rather than stored. A stored copy is stale the
  moment stock moves, and keeping it fresh would mean this module subscribing
  to inventory's events for a value it can work out from a record it already
  holds.
  """
  inStock: Boolean!
  "The values that place the variant in the option space (e.g. Size: S)."
  optionValues: [OptionValue!]!

  """
  The variant's price set — it is the PRICING module's record.

  It is untyped and that is a DECISION THAT WAS MADE. Typing its fields here
  would mean COPYING pricing's schema into product's schema: the two schemas
  would have to change at the same time, and when one is forgotten the
  storefront either asks for a field that does not exist or hides one that
  does. Because the record arrives in this module loosely typed from the Query
  layer (see service.StoreVariant), typing it here would not be a CONVERSION
  but a CLAIM.

  The cost is clear and has been accepted: the client cannot learn the field
  names from the schema, it looks at pricing's documentation. In return two
  things are gained — when pricing adds a field it reaches the client without
  the storefront schema changing, and if pricing is NOT installed the field
  returns null. A typed field would in that case have to invent a "product with
  a price of zero"; showing no price at all is better than showing a wrong one.
  """
  priceSet: JSON

  """
  The variant's inventory item — it is the INVENTORY module's record.

  The rationale for it being untyped is the same as for priceSet.
  """
  inventoryItem: JSON
}

"The product's option axis (e.g. Size)."
type Option {
  id: ID!
  productId: ID!
  title: String!
  rank: Int!
  values: [OptionValue!]!
}

"A single value of an option (e.g. S)."
type OptionValue {
  id: ID!
  optionId: ID!
  value: String!
  rank: Int!
  """
  The title of the option the value belongs to.

  It is filled in only on a variant's option values ("S" on its own is
  meaningless, "Size: S" is meaningful); under Option.values it is an EMPTY
  STRING.
  """
  optionTitle: String!
}

"A product image; the file itself does not live in this module, only its address is stored."
type Image {
  id: ID!
  productId: ID!
  url: String!
  """
  What the picture SHOWS, for a reader who cannot see it.

  An empty string is a real answer rather than a missing one: HTML gives alt=""
  the meaning "this image carries no information", which is what a decorative
  picture is. The field is therefore non-null.
  """
  altText: String!
  rank: Int!
  metadata: JSON
}

"A free-form tag attached to the product."
type Tag {
  id: ID!
  value: String!
}

"A category that groups products in a tree shape."
type Category {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  handle: String!
  description: String
  parentId: ID
  isActive: Boolean!
  isInternal: Boolean!
  rank: Int!
}

"""
The order a product listing comes back in.

The values are spelled exactly as the REST surface spells them, and lower case
rather than the SCREAMING_CASE GraphQL convention, because the enum is BOUND to
the module's own type (see gqlgen.yml): a second spelling would need a converter,
and a converter is the second definition this schema's binding policy exists to
avoid.
"""
enum ProductOrder {
  newest
  oldest
}
